WEBVTT

1
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,382
[crickets chirping]

2
00:00:20,354 --> 00:00:22,857
[clatter]

3
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:30,832
[gentle music]

4
00:01:17,745 --> 00:01:20,582
It looks like a painting.

5
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:21,916
It doesn't even seem real.

6
00:01:29,724 --> 00:01:32,159
[footsteps approaching]

7
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:40,902
GIRL: Masie!

8
00:01:40,935 --> 00:01:43,270
[suspenseful music]

9
00:01:44,672 --> 00:01:45,640
Maisie!

10
00:01:45,673 --> 00:01:48,141
[metallic rattle]

11
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:51,646
[music continues]

12
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:07,028
Cricket!

13
00:02:07,061 --> 00:02:09,496
[breathing heavily]

14
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:14,969
Where's dad?

15
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:19,907
[suspenseful music]

16
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:20,842
Don't look at it.

17
00:03:06,186 --> 00:03:09,057
Is he gone?

18
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:11,693
He probably just
ran into town.

19
00:03:11,726 --> 00:03:12,527
I'm hungry.

20
00:03:14,729 --> 00:03:15,530
OK.

21
00:03:30,144 --> 00:03:31,846
Is that a new story?

22
00:03:31,879 --> 00:03:34,281
I'm working.

23
00:03:34,314 --> 00:03:35,983
What's it about?

24
00:03:36,017 --> 00:03:38,418
It's about a fire that
jumps out of a barrel

25
00:03:38,452 --> 00:03:39,987
and chases two sisters.

26
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:41,556
I'm pretty sure
they're going to die.

27
00:03:44,291 --> 00:03:46,493
Cricket, we
haven't prayed yet.

28
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:50,064
You pray for an hour.

29
00:03:50,098 --> 00:03:53,034
Lord, I thank you
for this breakfast.

30
00:03:53,067 --> 00:03:57,270
And I thank you for
my sister, who I love.

31
00:03:57,304 --> 00:03:59,107
Lord, I pray for our brother.

32
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:02,342
Help him to not get lost
out there in the desert.

33
00:04:02,375 --> 00:04:05,012
I pray that dad can get
home before his biscuits get

34
00:04:05,046 --> 00:04:06,180
too cold.

35
00:04:06,214 --> 00:04:08,816
And Lord, please keep
the flies from biting

36
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:10,383
Darlene too much this summer.

37
00:04:42,482 --> 00:04:44,952
[distant bird squawking]

38
00:05:08,810 --> 00:05:10,477
CRICKET: Maisie!

39
00:05:10,511 --> 00:05:11,311
Maisie!

40
00:05:14,048 --> 00:05:16,483
[birds chirping]

41
00:05:20,353 --> 00:05:22,223
Cricket, you
got to hold still.

42
00:05:31,532 --> 00:05:32,332
Maisie.

43
00:05:32,365 --> 00:05:33,868
Uh-huh?

44
00:05:33,901 --> 00:05:35,102
You look like mom.

45
00:05:41,474 --> 00:05:43,945
[CRICKETS CHIPRING, BIRDS
CHIRPING]

46
00:05:57,225 --> 00:06:01,028
You're not supposed
to go in there.

47
00:06:01,062 --> 00:06:02,997
Dad left it open.

48
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:03,831
Come on.

49
00:06:08,102 --> 00:06:10,605
[wooden creaking]

50
00:06:20,581 --> 00:06:22,850
What is this place?

51
00:06:22,884 --> 00:06:24,952
It's our sugarhouse.

52
00:06:24,986 --> 00:06:27,521
You don't remember?

53
00:06:27,555 --> 00:06:30,124
We used to come here all
the time during winter

54
00:06:30,157 --> 00:06:32,994
and make syrup.

55
00:06:33,027 --> 00:06:34,195
Why don't we do it anymore?

56
00:06:34,228 --> 00:06:36,764
[melancholy music]

57
00:06:36,797 --> 00:06:37,932
'Cause mom died.

58
00:07:25,746 --> 00:07:28,215
[car approaching]

59
00:07:29,717 --> 00:07:33,220
[ominous music]

60
00:08:21,635 --> 00:08:24,105
[engine whirring]

61
00:08:26,607 --> 00:08:29,110
[car door opens, closes]

62
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:33,748
Hi there.

63
00:08:33,781 --> 00:08:35,416
Are you Maisie Sire?

64
00:08:35,449 --> 00:08:36,817
Yes, sir.

65
00:08:36,851 --> 00:08:39,387
Is your dad Gage Sire?

66
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:40,488
Is he in trouble?

67
00:08:40,521 --> 00:08:41,555
No, he's not in trouble.

68
00:08:41,589 --> 00:08:45,259
I just-- I just
need to talk to him.

69
00:08:45,292 --> 00:08:47,061
Is that your sister there?

70
00:08:47,094 --> 00:08:49,330
Yes, sir, that's Cricket.

71
00:08:49,363 --> 00:08:53,134
OFFICER: Who, uh-- who takes
care of you in Cricket?

72
00:08:53,167 --> 00:08:55,603
My dad.

73
00:08:55,636 --> 00:08:58,172
And if for some reason,
he wouldn't come home?

74
00:09:00,775 --> 00:09:03,044
The reason I ask, because
of your age, you two

75
00:09:03,077 --> 00:09:04,545
would have to go
into foster care.

76
00:09:07,214 --> 00:09:09,050
Would they split us up?

77
00:09:09,083 --> 00:09:11,285
I'm not sure what
their policy is.

78
00:09:15,122 --> 00:09:16,490
Cricket's adopted.

79
00:09:16,525 --> 00:09:18,259
Does that matter?

80
00:09:18,292 --> 00:09:21,562
I don't know how they handle
that type of situation either.

81
00:09:21,595 --> 00:09:23,831
Would you possibly
have any other family

82
00:09:23,864 --> 00:09:26,267
that you could live with or
that could look after you two?

83
00:09:30,738 --> 00:09:32,541
We have a brother, Finn.

84
00:09:32,573 --> 00:09:34,175
He could look after us.

85
00:09:34,208 --> 00:09:36,777
OK, I'm familiar with Finn.

86
00:09:36,811 --> 00:09:40,381
Um, I'll stop back by in a
couple of days and touch base

87
00:09:40,414 --> 00:09:41,782
again.

88
00:09:41,816 --> 00:09:43,150
Maybe your dad will be back, OK?

89
00:09:48,389 --> 00:09:50,891
[car door opens, closes]

90
00:09:52,359 --> 00:09:54,862
[engine starts, car revs]

91
00:10:03,304 --> 00:10:05,773
[insects chirping]

92
00:10:23,691 --> 00:10:25,659
[phone chimes]

93
00:10:29,130 --> 00:10:30,631
[phone clicks]

94
00:10:38,573 --> 00:10:41,075
[coyotes howling]

95
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:51,118
[howls]

96
00:10:52,521 --> 00:10:54,989
[howling continues]

97
00:10:59,994 --> 00:11:02,496
[clatter]

98
00:11:36,931 --> 00:11:38,899
[fridge door closes]

99
00:11:38,933 --> 00:11:41,402
[leaves rustling]

100
00:12:03,958 --> 00:12:06,327
Cricket!

101
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:09,830
[gentle music]

102
00:12:29,183 --> 00:12:31,653
[thunder cracks, rumbles]

103
00:12:47,101 --> 00:12:49,537
Cricket, there's a storm coming.

104
00:12:49,571 --> 00:12:52,674
We need to get in, hon!

105
00:12:52,707 --> 00:12:54,542
Don't make me come down there!

106
00:12:58,445 --> 00:12:59,246
Cricket!

107
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:15,829
[thunder rumbles]

108
00:13:18,299 --> 00:13:20,735
[rain pouring]

109
00:13:28,208 --> 00:13:30,444
I love you to the moon.

110
00:13:30,477 --> 00:13:32,313
I love you to
the grocery store.

111
00:13:32,346 --> 00:13:35,949
That's better than last night.

112
00:13:35,983 --> 00:13:37,552
Love you to the toilet.

113
00:13:37,585 --> 00:13:38,720
[chuckles]

114
00:13:38,753 --> 00:13:40,154
Goodnight.

115
00:13:40,187 --> 00:13:41,155
Goodnight.

116
00:13:46,393 --> 00:13:47,961
MAISIE: Do you
remember when mom would

117
00:13:47,995 --> 00:13:49,731
stay up with us during storms?

118
00:13:56,470 --> 00:13:57,271
I do.

119
00:14:00,508 --> 00:14:04,311
I remember like
it was yesterday.

120
00:14:04,345 --> 00:14:06,681
[thunder cracks, rumbles]

121
00:14:07,816 --> 00:14:10,117
Cricket, can I stay in
here with you tonight?

122
00:14:23,430 --> 00:14:26,934
[gentle music]

123
00:15:18,318 --> 00:15:20,822
[flies buzzing]

124
00:16:18,245 --> 00:16:21,649
Did you know that cows cry?

125
00:16:21,683 --> 00:16:25,520
It says that when a calf is
taken away from its mother,

126
00:16:25,553 --> 00:16:26,987
the cow will grieve For?

127
00:16:27,020 --> 00:16:28,790
Weeks.

128
00:16:28,823 --> 00:16:32,727
She will have a mournful,
high-pitched cry

129
00:16:32,760 --> 00:16:35,028
and even have tears in her eyes.

130
00:16:46,406 --> 00:16:48,342
Isn't that the saddest
thing you've ever heard?

131
00:16:58,151 --> 00:17:00,087
[car approaching]

132
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,625
[suspenseful music]

133
00:17:47,902 --> 00:17:50,370
Dad?

134
00:17:50,404 --> 00:17:52,439
Hey, girlie, where's it at?

135
00:18:03,150 --> 00:18:06,353
What are you doing?

136
00:18:06,386 --> 00:18:08,923
Got nothing to do with you.

137
00:18:08,957 --> 00:18:11,158
Best go and get Gage, come
out and open up this hood.

138
00:18:23,972 --> 00:18:24,839
He ain't here, is he?

139
00:18:28,342 --> 00:18:29,611
Reckon you wish he was.

140
00:18:35,516 --> 00:18:37,018
Got your stuffed
lamb running loose.

141
00:18:39,587 --> 00:18:41,556
Cricket get inside!

142
00:18:41,589 --> 00:18:43,992
[breathing heavily]

143
00:18:44,926 --> 00:18:46,193
Go on, handle your business.

144
00:18:49,030 --> 00:18:50,230
We'll just take what we want.

145
00:19:04,478 --> 00:19:05,312
Don't rush me, Floyd.

146
00:19:05,345 --> 00:19:08,382
You think I can't smell it.

147
00:19:08,415 --> 00:19:10,283
[metallic bang]

148
00:19:12,587 --> 00:19:14,388
Come on, go.

149
00:19:14,421 --> 00:19:16,724
[breathing heavily]

150
00:19:18,559 --> 00:19:19,426
Closet, go.

151
00:19:23,965 --> 00:19:25,066
CRICKET: What's happening?

152
00:19:25,099 --> 00:19:25,900
I can't see.

153
00:19:38,478 --> 00:19:39,514
QUAID: No, I will find it.

154
00:19:39,547 --> 00:19:42,016
I'm going to find him.

155
00:19:42,050 --> 00:19:43,818
CRICKET: Are you going
to call the police?

156
00:19:47,454 --> 00:19:49,924
[muffled conversation]

157
00:19:53,393 --> 00:19:55,897
Maisie, I'm scared.

158
00:19:55,930 --> 00:19:57,765
Can you come sit with me?

159
00:19:57,799 --> 00:19:59,332
[rattling]

160
00:19:59,366 --> 00:20:00,134
QUAID: Over there.

161
00:20:00,168 --> 00:20:01,301
I don't care where they are.

162
00:20:01,334 --> 00:20:04,539
[tense music]

163
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:13,614
Did you call?

164
00:20:17,585 --> 00:20:18,920
They'll take you away from me.

165
00:20:21,622 --> 00:20:23,925
[muffled conversation continues]

166
00:20:27,260 --> 00:20:31,298
Dear God, please
make them go away.

167
00:20:31,331 --> 00:20:32,200
Make them go away.

168
00:20:32,232 --> 00:20:34,669
Make them go away.

169
00:20:34,702 --> 00:20:35,636
Make them go away.

170
00:20:51,318 --> 00:20:52,787
[car doors close]

171
00:20:52,820 --> 00:20:55,288
[engine starts]

172
00:20:59,227 --> 00:21:02,697
[vehicle receding]

173
00:21:06,701 --> 00:21:08,703
Are they gone?

174
00:21:08,736 --> 00:21:09,604
I think so.

175
00:21:21,582 --> 00:21:22,650
What if they come back?

176
00:21:38,166 --> 00:21:39,867
Who are you calling?

177
00:21:39,901 --> 00:21:40,701
Finn.

178
00:21:44,572 --> 00:21:48,075
[gentle music]

179
00:21:53,047 --> 00:21:56,517
[animals chirping]

180
00:22:00,521 --> 00:22:02,990
[bird squawking]

181
00:23:16,197 --> 00:23:18,199
[phone chimes]

182
00:23:57,038 --> 00:24:00,541
[gentle music]

183
00:24:16,223 --> 00:24:17,024
CRICKET: Maisie!

184
00:24:27,668 --> 00:24:28,468
Maisie!

185
00:24:58,399 --> 00:25:01,302
[chatter, laughter]

186
00:25:33,034 --> 00:25:34,235
I want macaroni.

187
00:25:36,971 --> 00:25:37,772
In a minute.

188
00:25:47,148 --> 00:25:49,650
[tv noise]

189
00:25:51,819 --> 00:25:52,620
Ow.

190
00:25:56,624 --> 00:25:58,559
MAN (ON TV): Hey, get back here.

191
00:25:58,592 --> 00:26:00,828
Don't worry, ma'am,
I'm a professional.

192
00:26:03,798 --> 00:26:04,698
Ow.

193
00:26:04,732 --> 00:26:05,599
Oh, sorry.

194
00:26:09,904 --> 00:26:12,339
[chatter]

195
00:26:23,184 --> 00:26:25,753
What are you doing?

196
00:26:25,786 --> 00:26:27,688
Playing hopscotch.

197
00:26:27,721 --> 00:26:28,856
That's not how you play.

198
00:26:32,927 --> 00:26:34,061
You're weird.

199
00:26:34,095 --> 00:26:36,497
[whistle blows]

200
00:26:36,531 --> 00:26:39,967
[melancholy music]

201
00:26:48,843 --> 00:26:49,643
Cricket!

202
00:26:52,980 --> 00:26:53,781
Cricket!

203
00:27:11,398 --> 00:27:12,233
Do you need a tow?

204
00:27:38,993 --> 00:27:39,793
Maisie.

205
00:28:01,715 --> 00:28:02,683
[thud]

206
00:28:02,716 --> 00:28:03,684
[cricket screams]

207
00:28:03,717 --> 00:28:04,519
Cricket!

208
00:28:09,456 --> 00:28:10,424
FINN: I got to see that.

209
00:28:10,457 --> 00:28:12,259
Oh, yeah, it's just so much.

210
00:28:12,293 --> 00:28:14,962
I can't even tell you
how much I've learned.

211
00:28:14,995 --> 00:28:16,598
Hey, Maisie Mae.

212
00:28:16,631 --> 00:28:17,965
You want to hug a ghost?

213
00:28:21,869 --> 00:28:23,572
You about made
my heart jump out.

214
00:28:23,605 --> 00:28:24,639
You know that, right?

215
00:28:24,673 --> 00:28:28,008
What do you want with a heart?

216
00:28:28,042 --> 00:28:28,809
Mm.

217
00:28:28,842 --> 00:28:30,244
I'm glad you're home.

218
00:28:30,277 --> 00:28:32,213
Oh, well, we'll
see about that.

219
00:28:34,882 --> 00:28:36,850
I've got a truckload
of dead bodies.

220
00:28:36,884 --> 00:28:37,686
You want to see?

221
00:28:44,058 --> 00:28:46,360
Wow.

222
00:28:46,393 --> 00:28:47,795
Where did you get all this?

223
00:28:47,828 --> 00:28:50,231
Oh, that first
house off the highway.

224
00:28:50,264 --> 00:28:52,266
Seriously, Finn?

225
00:28:52,299 --> 00:28:54,301
They shouldn't
have left it outside.

226
00:28:54,335 --> 00:28:55,903
Where are we going to put that?

227
00:28:55,936 --> 00:28:57,004
Crawling on the house.

228
00:28:57,037 --> 00:28:57,838
Nice.

229
00:29:00,675 --> 00:29:01,643
Oh, wait.

230
00:29:01,676 --> 00:29:02,743
I need a picture.

231
00:29:02,776 --> 00:29:03,545
Hang on.

232
00:29:03,578 --> 00:29:04,912
[exhales deeply]

233
00:29:06,780 --> 00:29:09,116
[blows raspberries]

234
00:29:09,149 --> 00:29:11,485
[mimics choking]

235
00:29:11,519 --> 00:29:13,320
CRICKET: Take it already.

236
00:29:13,354 --> 00:29:14,955
Finn, look normal.

237
00:29:14,989 --> 00:29:16,991
This is normal.

238
00:29:17,024 --> 00:29:18,826
Come on!

239
00:29:18,859 --> 00:29:20,060
OK, got it.

240
00:29:32,707 --> 00:29:36,176
[cricket chirping, dog barking]

241
00:29:54,562 --> 00:29:55,396
How about that?

242
00:30:00,635 --> 00:30:02,303
How about that?

243
00:30:02,336 --> 00:30:04,739
You can't jump three
in a row like that.

244
00:30:04,773 --> 00:30:05,740
That's not in the rules.

245
00:30:05,774 --> 00:30:06,741
Yes, I can.

246
00:30:06,775 --> 00:30:07,642
No, you can't.

247
00:30:07,676 --> 00:30:09,143
- Yes, I can.
- No, you can't.

248
00:30:09,176 --> 00:30:09,977
Yes, I can.

249
00:30:10,010 --> 00:30:10,779
Says who?

250
00:30:10,811 --> 00:30:11,613
Says the cow.

251
00:30:11,646 --> 00:30:12,413
Oh.

252
00:30:12,446 --> 00:30:16,050
[giggles]

253
00:30:25,527 --> 00:30:27,861
I don't mind
sleeping on the couch.

254
00:30:27,895 --> 00:30:29,096
It's OK.

255
00:30:29,129 --> 00:30:31,165
I put some more
hangers in the closet,

256
00:30:31,198 --> 00:30:33,768
and I just cleared
out the top drawer.

257
00:30:33,802 --> 00:30:37,572
Oh, and look at this.

258
00:30:37,605 --> 00:30:39,507
Mr. Gill.

259
00:30:39,541 --> 00:30:42,142
[chuckles] I found him
in the attic for you.

260
00:30:48,182 --> 00:30:49,517
I wanted you to feel at home.

261
00:31:07,935 --> 00:31:10,839
[pensive music]

262
00:31:10,871 --> 00:31:12,906
Did you hear from Dad?

263
00:31:12,940 --> 00:31:16,410
FINN: Just you.

264
00:31:16,443 --> 00:31:19,413
Where do you think he is?

265
00:31:19,446 --> 00:31:21,382
Out looking for something.

266
00:31:30,224 --> 00:31:34,361
Um, Cricket was wanting to
go pick pumpkins tomorrow.

267
00:31:34,395 --> 00:31:37,832
It'd be nice, us doing
family stuff again.

268
00:31:37,866 --> 00:31:39,299
Sure.

269
00:31:39,333 --> 00:31:40,300
Awesome.

270
00:31:42,871 --> 00:31:45,038
OK, well, I'll let you sleep.

271
00:31:45,072 --> 00:31:46,473
Good night, Finn.

272
00:31:46,508 --> 00:31:48,375
Night.

273
00:31:48,409 --> 00:31:51,813
[gentle music]

274
00:31:52,781 --> 00:31:56,183
[insects chirping]

275
00:31:58,620 --> 00:32:02,022
[groans]

276
00:32:03,490 --> 00:32:04,425
[pants]

277
00:32:04,458 --> 00:32:06,628
Hey, Finn, look.

278
00:32:06,661 --> 00:32:07,494
I found mine.

279
00:32:07,529 --> 00:32:09,496
Looks just like you.

280
00:32:09,531 --> 00:32:11,633
Where's yours?

281
00:32:11,666 --> 00:32:13,233
Right there.

282
00:32:13,267 --> 00:32:14,435
[flies buzzing]

283
00:32:14,468 --> 00:32:16,036
MAISIE: You can't pick that one.

284
00:32:16,069 --> 00:32:17,070
FINN: Why not?

285
00:32:17,104 --> 00:32:19,641
Because why would
anyone choose that?

286
00:32:22,409 --> 00:32:25,212
Hey, Cricket, come help
me find Finn a pumpkin.

287
00:32:25,245 --> 00:32:27,882
CRICKET: OK.

288
00:32:27,916 --> 00:32:31,385
[insects chirping]

289
00:32:50,137 --> 00:32:53,106
[gulping]

290
00:33:04,953 --> 00:33:07,454
Hey, are you giving
away my pumpkin seeds?

291
00:33:07,488 --> 00:33:10,390
[drink dribbling]

292
00:33:13,327 --> 00:33:15,563
She just licked
them a little bit.

293
00:33:15,597 --> 00:33:17,297
I know where your heart is.

294
00:33:17,331 --> 00:33:20,300
[chuckles]

295
00:33:21,134 --> 00:33:23,738
Finn, remember, this is my God.

296
00:33:23,771 --> 00:33:24,939
This is my family.

297
00:33:24,973 --> 00:33:25,940
And they are my heart.

298
00:33:25,974 --> 00:33:30,678
[chuckles] Mom
taught you that, right?

299
00:33:30,712 --> 00:33:34,582
She taught you too.

300
00:33:34,616 --> 00:33:37,184
Remember those
rabbits she got for you?

301
00:33:37,217 --> 00:33:39,319
Oh, my gosh, they
were so precious.

302
00:33:39,353 --> 00:33:42,122
They tore holes in
all of the carpets.

303
00:33:42,155 --> 00:33:44,826
That one tore a
hole in the TV cord.

304
00:33:44,859 --> 00:33:47,027
And she made them
the cutest little hats.

305
00:33:47,060 --> 00:33:48,897
She made hats for the rabbits?

306
00:33:48,930 --> 00:33:52,132
More like bonnets, but
they looked so sweet.

307
00:33:52,165 --> 00:33:56,036
[scoffs]

308
00:33:57,539 --> 00:33:59,774
Every time Dad
saw a rabbit, he'd

309
00:33:59,807 --> 00:34:02,577
tell me to go fetch a shovel.

310
00:34:02,610 --> 00:34:06,014
Dad liked my rabbits.

311
00:34:06,046 --> 00:34:08,115
You always think like that.

312
00:34:08,148 --> 00:34:10,818
Like what?

313
00:34:10,852 --> 00:34:11,753
Nothing.

314
00:34:15,122 --> 00:34:18,425
How do you think?

315
00:34:18,458 --> 00:34:19,661
I don't.

316
00:34:24,498 --> 00:34:25,567
I'm just a shovel.

317
00:34:28,201 --> 00:34:31,639
[birdsong]

318
00:34:33,575 --> 00:34:36,343
MAISIE: Finn, look.

319
00:34:36,376 --> 00:34:38,580
It's God's fingers.

320
00:34:38,613 --> 00:34:42,149
Wh-- what are
you talking about?

321
00:34:42,182 --> 00:34:43,551
Don't you see it?

322
00:34:43,585 --> 00:34:46,821
[creaking]

323
00:34:49,289 --> 00:34:51,626
I'm not wearing this again.

324
00:34:51,659 --> 00:34:54,194
Oh, you look adorable.

325
00:34:54,227 --> 00:34:56,030
I've worn this
since I was four.

326
00:34:56,064 --> 00:34:59,801
MAISIE: Well, we can put some
black leggings underneath.

327
00:34:59,834 --> 00:35:03,236
Oh, come on, Cricket.

328
00:35:03,270 --> 00:35:05,540
It's time to get rid
of that old sheepskin.

329
00:35:05,573 --> 00:35:07,474
Finally.

330
00:35:07,508 --> 00:35:11,311
It's time to run
with the wolves.

331
00:35:11,345 --> 00:35:14,281
[imitating wolves howling]

332
00:35:28,596 --> 00:35:30,130
All right, she's ready.

333
00:35:35,369 --> 00:35:37,371
She can't go like that.

334
00:35:37,404 --> 00:35:38,405
CRICKET: Why not?

335
00:35:38,438 --> 00:35:39,841
She's a devil.

336
00:35:39,874 --> 00:35:41,075
That's right.

337
00:35:41,109 --> 00:35:43,978
[snarling]

338
00:35:44,012 --> 00:35:47,649
Hey, come on, Cricket.

339
00:35:47,682 --> 00:35:48,850
Wait.

340
00:35:48,883 --> 00:35:49,651
Can I come?

341
00:35:49,684 --> 00:35:51,485
What are you supposed to be?

342
00:35:54,321 --> 00:35:56,124
A veterinarian.

343
00:35:56,156 --> 00:35:59,560
[sighs] I-- I don't think so.

344
00:35:59,594 --> 00:36:01,663
You could stay here
and pass out candy.

345
00:36:01,696 --> 00:36:06,100
We don't have candy,
because nobody ever comes.

346
00:36:08,970 --> 00:36:10,437
[dog barking]

347
00:36:10,470 --> 00:36:13,941
[spooky music]

348
00:36:16,911 --> 00:36:18,378
[growling]

349
00:36:18,412 --> 00:36:20,848
[screams]

350
00:36:20,882 --> 00:36:24,852
[chuckles]

351
00:36:28,388 --> 00:36:29,423
Hey, how about this one?

352
00:36:29,456 --> 00:36:31,059
The lights are off.

353
00:36:31,092 --> 00:36:32,426
So?

354
00:36:32,459 --> 00:36:35,262
If the lights are off,
you're not supposed to knock.

355
00:36:35,295 --> 00:36:36,764
It's the rules.

356
00:36:36,798 --> 00:36:40,702
Halloween's got no rules.

357
00:36:40,735 --> 00:36:41,536
[sighs]

358
00:36:41,569 --> 00:36:42,369
Here.

359
00:36:59,987 --> 00:37:03,423
[knocking on door]

360
00:37:06,928 --> 00:37:08,361
Trick or treat?

361
00:37:08,395 --> 00:37:10,198
I don't have
nothing to give you.

362
00:37:10,230 --> 00:37:10,998
Can't you see?

363
00:37:11,032 --> 00:37:11,999
The light's not on.

364
00:37:18,039 --> 00:37:21,576
You're gonna send my little
sister home without a treat?

365
00:37:21,609 --> 00:37:25,113
I told you I
don't have nothing.

366
00:37:25,146 --> 00:37:28,983
I said, "trick or treat?"

367
00:37:29,016 --> 00:37:32,285
What about that did
you not understand?

368
00:37:32,319 --> 00:37:34,188
I don't have nothing.

369
00:37:34,222 --> 00:37:35,890
I know.

370
00:37:35,923 --> 00:37:40,228
But it's Halloween,
so I got no choice.

371
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:44,198
See, I might just put
a mouse in your mailbox

372
00:37:44,232 --> 00:37:46,234
or maybe throw a rock
through a window.

373
00:37:48,903 --> 00:37:50,938
Maybe I'll set your car on fire.

374
00:37:54,441 --> 00:37:57,444
Yeah, that's the thing.

375
00:37:57,477 --> 00:37:58,478
You never know.

376
00:38:02,049 --> 00:38:04,952
I've got some butter
cookies in the cabinet.

377
00:38:04,986 --> 00:38:06,154
Now we're talking.

378
00:38:10,858 --> 00:38:13,661
Works every time.

379
00:38:13,694 --> 00:38:17,165
[pensive music]

380
00:38:27,675 --> 00:38:31,145
[flames crackling]

381
00:38:38,653 --> 00:38:42,123
[car engine humming]

382
00:38:56,103 --> 00:38:59,607
[grim music]

383
00:39:17,058 --> 00:39:20,061
[clacking]

384
00:39:47,487 --> 00:39:51,491
[sighs]

385
00:40:06,974 --> 00:40:10,443
[clacking]

386
00:40:23,925 --> 00:40:25,559
CRICKET: I'm not touching that.

387
00:40:25,593 --> 00:40:27,695
MAISIE: Well, my hand
won't fit in the drain.

388
00:40:34,869 --> 00:40:36,170
Yuck.

389
00:40:36,203 --> 00:40:38,706
[water sloshing]

390
00:40:38,739 --> 00:40:40,608
Can you feel it?

391
00:40:40,641 --> 00:40:42,009
I can't find it.

392
00:40:45,012 --> 00:40:46,013
I'm stuck!

393
00:40:46,047 --> 00:40:47,181
[screams]

394
00:40:47,214 --> 00:40:48,382
Finn!

395
00:40:48,416 --> 00:40:49,383
I'm stuck!

396
00:40:49,417 --> 00:40:50,184
Finn!

397
00:40:50,217 --> 00:40:51,385
Finn!

398
00:40:51,419 --> 00:40:54,388
Finn!

399
00:40:54,422 --> 00:40:57,892
[bird cawing]

400
00:41:24,719 --> 00:41:27,722
Cricket, time for breakfast.

401
00:41:27,755 --> 00:41:28,556
Finn.

402
00:41:35,896 --> 00:41:39,333
[pensive music]

403
00:41:49,777 --> 00:41:51,846
Finn.

404
00:41:51,879 --> 00:41:52,680
Finn.

405
00:42:00,621 --> 00:42:01,989
Check it out.

406
00:42:02,023 --> 00:42:03,391
Dad's old jacket.

407
00:42:03,424 --> 00:42:06,160
There's probably
spiders in it.

408
00:42:06,193 --> 00:42:09,030
This is tough.

409
00:42:09,063 --> 00:42:11,999
Finn, are you leaving?

410
00:42:12,033 --> 00:42:13,134
Yeah.

411
00:42:13,167 --> 00:42:16,337
Why?

412
00:42:16,370 --> 00:42:18,172
You've got things handled.

413
00:42:18,205 --> 00:42:20,274
No, I really don't.

414
00:42:20,307 --> 00:42:21,942
Dad will be back.

415
00:42:21,976 --> 00:42:24,612
But we need you here.

416
00:42:24,645 --> 00:42:28,315
Well, I need to
be somewhere else.

417
00:42:49,136 --> 00:42:52,606
[swing squeaking]

418
00:43:02,016 --> 00:43:03,217
Maisie.

419
00:43:03,250 --> 00:43:04,251
Mhm.

420
00:43:07,254 --> 00:43:10,758
[car engine humming]

421
00:43:12,726 --> 00:43:15,496
[ominous music]

422
00:43:15,530 --> 00:43:16,330
Go get Finn.

423
00:43:26,273 --> 00:43:29,110
Finn, they're here!

424
00:43:29,143 --> 00:43:30,579
Who's here?

425
00:43:30,611 --> 00:43:31,412
Them.

426
00:43:47,361 --> 00:43:50,297
[car engine stops]

427
00:43:51,265 --> 00:43:52,500
Hey there, chipmunk.

428
00:43:56,036 --> 00:43:57,671
You look like you
done seen the devil.

429
00:43:57,705 --> 00:43:58,507
MAISIE: Finn!

430
00:44:02,676 --> 00:44:04,613
Gage?

431
00:44:04,645 --> 00:44:06,147
Maisie, get inside

432
00:44:06,180 --> 00:44:07,516
Well, you ain't Gage.

433
00:44:07,549 --> 00:44:08,550
You Schmitz?

434
00:44:08,583 --> 00:44:09,917
You need to go.

435
00:44:13,087 --> 00:44:13,988
[thumps on door]

436
00:44:14,021 --> 00:44:16,223
You hear that, Floyd?

437
00:44:16,257 --> 00:44:17,626
Schmitz says we need to get.

438
00:44:24,431 --> 00:44:25,766
I have a feeling
like Floyd needs

439
00:44:25,799 --> 00:44:27,034
to stick around a little bit.

440
00:44:29,803 --> 00:44:32,740
We came here for
a package, wrapped

441
00:44:32,773 --> 00:44:34,942
up kind of like a sausage.

442
00:44:34,975 --> 00:44:36,877
Gage was the last
one who has seen it.

443
00:44:36,911 --> 00:44:38,412
Well, he's gone.

444
00:44:38,445 --> 00:44:40,748
QUAID: So maybe you
know something about it.

445
00:44:46,086 --> 00:44:47,354
What you hiding there, hoss?

446
00:44:51,859 --> 00:44:53,894
[cackles] Big man.

447
00:44:57,532 --> 00:44:59,099
Finn, get inside.

448
00:45:02,403 --> 00:45:04,171
There's nothing here for you.

449
00:45:04,205 --> 00:45:07,007
Where's my package?

450
00:45:07,041 --> 00:45:08,342
Ask Schmitz.

451
00:45:20,721 --> 00:45:22,189
Watch yourself, darling.

452
00:45:26,628 --> 00:45:29,129
He's got the smell of it on him.

453
00:45:29,163 --> 00:45:30,565
[car doors slamming]

454
00:45:30,599 --> 00:45:33,602
[car engine starts]

455
00:45:34,401 --> 00:45:35,436
All right.

456
00:45:44,679 --> 00:45:48,148
[solemn music]

457
00:46:32,226 --> 00:46:35,796
It's so quiet.

458
00:46:35,829 --> 00:46:38,733
Mom always said to
listen to the birds,

459
00:46:38,767 --> 00:46:41,935
because God takes care of them,
and he'll take care of you too.

460
00:46:46,173 --> 00:46:47,374
But I can't hear a thing.

461
00:46:51,145 --> 00:46:54,481
[food package rumpling]

462
00:46:57,851 --> 00:47:00,954
Cricket, seriously, use a bowl.

463
00:47:00,988 --> 00:47:03,792
Finn says I don't need a bowl.

464
00:47:03,824 --> 00:47:05,359
It's all going in my mouth.

465
00:47:12,066 --> 00:47:13,802
What happened to your hair?

466
00:47:13,834 --> 00:47:16,036
I'm going natural.

467
00:47:16,070 --> 00:47:18,038
Did Finn teach you that too?

468
00:47:22,876 --> 00:47:26,313
[munching]

469
00:47:33,187 --> 00:47:37,291
[groans] I don't
want the light.

470
00:47:37,324 --> 00:47:39,661
Finn, did you see this?

471
00:47:39,694 --> 00:47:42,396
I'm sleeping.

472
00:47:42,429 --> 00:47:47,434
Well, it says that we
owe tax on the house.

473
00:47:47,468 --> 00:47:51,238
I thought we only
paid tax at the store.

474
00:47:51,271 --> 00:47:52,707
Finn, can you please help?

475
00:47:52,741 --> 00:47:54,609
I don't know what to do.

476
00:47:54,642 --> 00:47:58,078
[finn groans]

477
00:48:03,484 --> 00:48:06,920
[car engine humming]

478
00:48:11,358 --> 00:48:12,326
[chatter]

479
00:48:12,359 --> 00:48:13,160
Ow.

480
00:48:34,448 --> 00:48:37,951
[school bell ringing]

481
00:48:43,424 --> 00:48:48,295
Maisie, come on in.

482
00:48:48,328 --> 00:48:51,800
So senior year, very exciting.

483
00:48:51,833 --> 00:48:53,200
Yes, ma'am.

484
00:48:53,233 --> 00:48:56,003
Are you looking forward
to your senior trip?

485
00:48:56,036 --> 00:49:00,174
Lake of the Ozarks
should be a blast.

486
00:49:00,207 --> 00:49:01,074
Please have a seat.

487
00:49:04,579 --> 00:49:07,347
So we're here to
talk about college.

488
00:49:07,381 --> 00:49:08,716
Yes, ma'am.

489
00:49:08,750 --> 00:49:11,051
I brought my application.

490
00:49:11,084 --> 00:49:15,222
Oh, you wanna
be a veterinarian.

491
00:49:15,255 --> 00:49:16,758
You must be an animal lover.

492
00:49:16,791 --> 00:49:19,694
Oh, my gosh, yes, even
before I could walk.

493
00:49:19,727 --> 00:49:20,795
TEACHER: Is that so?

494
00:49:20,829 --> 00:49:23,297
My mom always said
I was half rabbit.

495
00:49:23,330 --> 00:49:25,999
[chuckles] Oh,
that's adorable.

496
00:49:29,336 --> 00:49:34,041
Maisie, you know, it's not
easy to get into vet school.

497
00:49:34,074 --> 00:49:36,811
I know my grades aren't
the best right now.

498
00:49:36,845 --> 00:49:40,515
It's expensive too.

499
00:49:40,548 --> 00:49:42,216
Do your parents
have a college fund?

500
00:49:48,355 --> 00:49:52,125
Maisie, can I be
straight with you?

501
00:49:52,159 --> 00:49:55,395
If you wanna go to
college, you need

502
00:49:55,429 --> 00:49:57,632
to get a scholarship,
which means

503
00:49:57,665 --> 00:49:59,667
you need to get your grades up.

504
00:49:59,701 --> 00:50:00,802
OK.

505
00:50:00,835 --> 00:50:03,938
It means more
time on homework.

506
00:50:03,972 --> 00:50:06,608
It means after-school tutoring.

507
00:50:06,641 --> 00:50:11,044
And you need to find
ways to stand out.

508
00:50:11,078 --> 00:50:16,149
Maybe you could volunteer at
an animal shelter on weekends.

509
00:50:16,183 --> 00:50:18,385
I don't have a
lot of free time.

510
00:50:18,418 --> 00:50:23,190
Maisie, this is
your dream, right?

511
00:50:23,223 --> 00:50:25,325
Yes, ma'am.

512
00:50:25,359 --> 00:50:28,730
TEACHER: What are you
willing to sacrifice for it?

513
00:50:33,568 --> 00:50:37,037
[chair scraping]

514
00:50:39,039 --> 00:50:42,510
[hinges squealing]

515
00:51:01,428 --> 00:51:05,800
Cricket, let me
help you with that.

516
00:51:12,640 --> 00:51:13,575
[bell tolls]

517
00:51:13,608 --> 00:51:16,578
[dog barking]

518
00:51:16,611 --> 00:51:20,080
[metal clanking]

519
00:51:35,029 --> 00:51:37,497
[clacking]

520
00:51:37,532 --> 00:51:40,300
Coob?

521
00:51:40,334 --> 00:51:43,037
Finn Saier.

522
00:51:43,071 --> 00:51:44,872
[groans] Oh, my God.

523
00:51:44,906 --> 00:51:48,910
[chuckles] I thought
you were your dad.

524
00:51:48,943 --> 00:51:51,846
[chuckles] I heard you
run off with the circus.

525
00:51:51,879 --> 00:51:54,448
[chuckles] Uh, who said that?

526
00:51:54,481 --> 00:51:56,751
I did, to a lot of people.

527
00:51:56,784 --> 00:51:57,785
[both laugh]

528
00:51:57,819 --> 00:52:00,420
Hey, there's been
worse said about me.

529
00:52:00,454 --> 00:52:03,524
Someone said you skipped
town with your old man.

530
00:52:03,558 --> 00:52:06,126
What else have you heard?

531
00:52:06,159 --> 00:52:07,729
All I know is.

532
00:52:11,532 --> 00:52:14,736
I can't get any
ice in this town.

533
00:52:14,769 --> 00:52:16,136
[grim music]

534
00:52:16,169 --> 00:52:18,238
Is that right?

535
00:52:18,271 --> 00:52:19,107
Yeah.

536
00:52:28,683 --> 00:52:31,519
I might help with that.

537
00:52:31,552 --> 00:52:34,856
[chuckles]

538
00:52:35,657 --> 00:52:36,456
Yeah.

539
00:53:05,419 --> 00:53:08,422
[whoosh]

540
00:53:19,399 --> 00:53:22,870
[car engine humming]

541
00:53:58,305 --> 00:54:01,308
[coins rattling]

542
00:54:02,309 --> 00:54:03,511
Sorry.

543
00:54:03,544 --> 00:54:04,879
We're not open.

544
00:54:04,912 --> 00:54:07,548
Can I get a job?

545
00:54:07,582 --> 00:54:10,752
Why aren't you in school?

546
00:54:10,785 --> 00:54:12,720
Because I'm done with it.

547
00:54:45,820 --> 00:54:49,289
[car engine humming]

548
00:54:55,263 --> 00:54:58,733
[christmas carol]

549
00:55:32,033 --> 00:55:34,735
[door opens, closes]

550
00:55:34,769 --> 00:55:35,570
Hey.

551
00:55:39,707 --> 00:55:41,676
You put up the tree without me.

552
00:55:41,709 --> 00:55:45,345
I told him to wait.

553
00:55:45,378 --> 00:55:47,048
What happened to my ornament?

554
00:55:47,081 --> 00:55:48,749
CRICKET: Finn dropped it.

555
00:55:48,783 --> 00:55:50,383
How about this?

556
00:55:50,417 --> 00:55:54,322
I'll break one of mine,
and we'll be even, OK?

557
00:55:54,354 --> 00:55:55,223
[ornament cracks]

558
00:55:55,256 --> 00:55:56,423
I made that!

559
00:55:56,456 --> 00:55:59,627
[laughs]

560
00:56:00,661 --> 00:56:02,563
I'll just break two
of mine then, OK?

561
00:56:02,597 --> 00:56:07,500
No, just stop
breaking everything!

562
00:56:07,535 --> 00:56:09,270
[door slams]

563
00:56:09,303 --> 00:56:11,138
We should have
waited for Maisie.

564
00:56:21,082 --> 00:56:24,585
[insects chirping]

565
00:57:27,815 --> 00:57:31,319
[car engine starts]

566
00:57:34,789 --> 00:57:38,292
[vehicle leaving]

567
00:58:00,181 --> 00:58:04,185
[sighs]

568
00:58:10,157 --> 00:58:11,826
Aren't you gonna pray?

569
00:58:20,568 --> 00:58:24,205
God, I thank
you for Christmas.

570
00:58:24,238 --> 00:58:28,242
I thank you for this food,
and I thank you for cricket.

571
00:58:32,880 --> 00:58:38,786
And, God, I pray for Finn, even
though I don't feel like it.

572
00:58:38,819 --> 00:58:39,620
Amen.

573
00:58:49,263 --> 00:58:52,700
[wind howling]

574
00:59:05,446 --> 00:59:07,915
[clatter]

575
00:59:07,948 --> 00:59:11,052
Cricket, can you do
that outside, please?

576
00:59:11,085 --> 00:59:12,119
It's cold.

577
00:59:21,862 --> 00:59:25,299
[grim music]

578
00:59:27,268 --> 00:59:28,202
Cricket.

579
00:59:40,881 --> 00:59:41,949
Cricket?

580
00:59:41,982 --> 00:59:45,820
[gasps]

581
00:59:50,124 --> 00:59:52,426
Where's my jumprope?

582
00:59:52,460 --> 00:59:55,530
It's in your hand.

583
00:59:55,564 --> 00:59:57,098
Here.

584
00:59:57,131 --> 00:59:57,932
See?

585
01:00:04,105 --> 01:00:05,272
Where's my jumprope?

586
01:00:19,286 --> 01:00:22,723
[car engine humming]

587
01:00:43,978 --> 01:00:46,447
Did I amaze you, Mai?

588
01:00:46,480 --> 01:00:47,549
Merry Christmas.

589
01:00:47,582 --> 01:00:49,518
We already had Christmas.

590
01:00:49,551 --> 01:00:50,451
It's over now.

591
01:00:50,484 --> 01:00:52,119
But I got you a present.

592
01:00:56,023 --> 01:00:58,659
Did you steal him
from the Nativity?

593
01:00:58,692 --> 01:01:00,161
Yeah, I did.

594
01:01:00,194 --> 01:01:02,363
It isn't funny, Finn.

595
01:01:02,396 --> 01:01:04,999
Cricket and I, we
opened presents,

596
01:01:05,032 --> 01:01:08,035
and we built a snowman.

597
01:01:08,068 --> 01:01:11,238
You missed it all.

598
01:01:11,272 --> 01:01:13,140
Come on.

599
01:01:13,174 --> 01:01:15,276
I got him for you.

600
01:01:15,309 --> 01:01:16,944
You should have
got him for yourself.

601
01:01:19,648 --> 01:01:21,115
[somber music]

602
01:01:21,148 --> 01:01:22,617
[door slams]

603
01:01:26,086 --> 01:01:30,024
[sighs]

604
01:01:50,711 --> 01:01:53,648
[chatter]

605
01:01:58,520 --> 01:01:59,954
[bell rings]

606
01:01:59,987 --> 01:02:02,323
All right, class,
let's get ready to go.

607
01:02:08,062 --> 01:02:09,263
GIRL: Bye, Ms. Patty.

608
01:02:09,296 --> 01:02:10,364
Bye.

609
01:02:10,397 --> 01:02:11,198
Bye.

610
01:02:13,167 --> 01:02:13,968
Cricket.

611
01:02:27,515 --> 01:02:28,315
Cricket?

612
01:02:31,620 --> 01:02:34,989
[phone vibrating, ringing]

613
01:02:38,359 --> 01:02:41,295
Maisie, no phones, please.

614
01:02:41,328 --> 01:02:42,196
Sorry.

615
01:03:00,649 --> 01:03:04,018
NURSE (ON PHONE): Yes, this
message is for Maisie Saier.

616
01:03:04,051 --> 01:03:05,786
I'm the nurse at
Eagle Elementary,

617
01:03:05,819 --> 01:03:10,124
and your sister, Cricket,
um, she just had a seizure.

618
01:03:10,157 --> 01:03:12,259
We've been trying to
reach your father, Gage,

619
01:03:12,293 --> 01:03:15,162
but he won't respond.

620
01:03:15,195 --> 01:03:16,531
Could you please
come right away?

621
01:03:16,564 --> 01:03:18,533
I can tell you more
when you arrive.

622
01:03:18,566 --> 01:03:19,433
All right, thank you.

623
01:03:19,466 --> 01:03:20,267
Bye.

624
01:03:25,039 --> 01:03:28,175
Hey, what did I
say about phones?

625
01:03:28,208 --> 01:03:29,577
[sighs] It's my sister.

626
01:03:29,611 --> 01:03:30,444
I need to get her.

627
01:03:30,477 --> 01:03:32,413
After the shift.

628
01:03:32,446 --> 01:03:33,247
Come on.

629
01:03:36,618 --> 01:03:37,552
[keypad tone]

630
01:03:37,585 --> 01:03:39,953
[sniffling]

631
01:03:39,987 --> 01:03:43,357
[line ringing]

632
01:03:44,793 --> 01:03:47,227
Come on, Finn.

633
01:03:47,261 --> 01:03:48,228
[machines whirring]

634
01:03:48,262 --> 01:03:51,733
[beeping]

635
01:03:59,139 --> 01:04:01,576
[shouts]

636
01:04:01,609 --> 01:04:05,012
[cackling]

637
01:04:05,045 --> 01:04:08,449
[phone ringing, vibrating]

638
01:04:17,726 --> 01:04:19,126
What are you now up to?

639
01:04:19,159 --> 01:04:21,929
Uh, it's cold.

640
01:04:21,962 --> 01:04:24,599
It is cold as a walrus's butt.

641
01:04:24,632 --> 01:04:25,399
That's what I know.

642
01:04:25,432 --> 01:04:27,101
[chuckles]

643
01:04:27,134 --> 01:04:30,538
I think a walrus's butt
would get pretty toasted.

644
01:04:30,572 --> 01:04:32,607
You wanna reach your
hand down there and check?

645
01:04:32,640 --> 01:04:33,474
No.

646
01:04:33,508 --> 01:04:34,475
[phone vibrating]

647
01:04:34,509 --> 01:04:37,712
That your girlfriend calling?

648
01:04:37,746 --> 01:04:38,847
It's nothing.

649
01:04:38,879 --> 01:04:40,914
Oh, come on.

650
01:04:40,948 --> 01:04:43,450
Pick it up, loverboy.

651
01:04:43,484 --> 01:04:45,953
Mwah, mwah, mwah, mwah.

652
01:04:45,986 --> 01:04:48,055
I could-- I could step outside.

653
01:04:48,088 --> 01:04:51,225
If the two of you wanna
climb in the back, I can--

654
01:04:51,258 --> 01:04:54,328
[shrieks] who's the baby?

655
01:04:54,361 --> 01:04:55,830
Chill.

656
01:04:55,864 --> 01:04:58,298
It's just a baby Jesus.

657
01:04:58,332 --> 01:05:02,537
That's the story, ain't it?

658
01:05:02,570 --> 01:05:05,272
You don't wanna hear it.

659
01:05:05,305 --> 01:05:07,709
Well, you need to
get it out of here.

660
01:05:07,742 --> 01:05:09,243
What?

661
01:05:09,276 --> 01:05:13,648
I ain't buying no glass
with baby Jesus staring at me.

662
01:05:16,885 --> 01:05:17,918
I ain't lying.

663
01:05:42,176 --> 01:05:44,278
Put something over his eyes!

664
01:05:44,311 --> 01:05:47,682
[sighs]

665
01:05:47,715 --> 01:05:51,084
[phone vibrating]

666
01:05:51,118 --> 01:05:55,857
Hey, loverboy, your
girl is calling again.

667
01:05:55,890 --> 01:05:58,793
[chuckles] Hey, come on.

668
01:06:03,497 --> 01:06:04,431
[door slams]

669
01:06:04,465 --> 01:06:07,902
[line ringing]

670
01:06:12,339 --> 01:06:16,845
Maisie, uh, we've got
tables that need bussing.

671
01:06:16,878 --> 01:06:17,679
Did you hear me?

672
01:06:26,487 --> 01:06:27,922
You're a quitter, aren't you?

673
01:06:31,258 --> 01:06:32,827
You don't know me.

674
01:06:42,269 --> 01:06:46,674
Automatism can happen
during a seizure.

675
01:06:46,708 --> 01:06:51,779
It's when you repeat a motion
without being aware of it.

676
01:06:51,813 --> 01:06:56,116
Have you noticed anything
unusual with Cricket, things

677
01:06:56,149 --> 01:07:00,153
like staring into space
or rapid blinking?

678
01:07:00,187 --> 01:07:04,759
On Christmas, it was,
like, she just froze up.

679
01:07:04,792 --> 01:07:07,494
Maisie, we need to have
Cricket see a neurologist.

680
01:07:07,529 --> 01:07:10,497
They would be able to
diagnose if this is epilepsy.

681
01:07:10,532 --> 01:07:11,566
Is that dangerous?

682
01:07:11,599 --> 01:07:13,968
It's treatable
with medication.

683
01:07:14,002 --> 01:07:16,403
She can still have
a normal life.

684
01:07:16,436 --> 01:07:21,776
But there's risk of
more serious seizures,

685
01:07:21,809 --> 01:07:24,812
and those can be scary.

686
01:07:24,846 --> 01:07:30,050
It's important to keep
Cricket away from stress.

687
01:07:30,083 --> 01:07:31,351
How have things been at home?

688
01:07:34,656 --> 01:07:38,125
[pensive music]

689
01:08:11,759 --> 01:08:15,228
[car engine humming]

690
01:08:28,076 --> 01:08:29,844
Do you remember this?

691
01:08:32,981 --> 01:08:33,815
Maybe.

692
01:08:37,018 --> 01:08:41,022
[yells]

693
01:08:47,494 --> 01:08:48,495
Maisie.

694
01:08:58,338 --> 01:08:59,373
Why did you call?

695
01:09:03,544 --> 01:09:09,550
Because Cricket had a
seizure, and I lost my job.

696
01:09:12,754 --> 01:09:15,123
And I can't go to
college anymore.

697
01:09:20,160 --> 01:09:21,596
None of that matters to you.

698
01:09:53,728 --> 01:09:57,230
[pensive music]

699
01:10:55,489 --> 01:10:58,960
[car engine humming]

700
01:11:10,437 --> 01:11:12,540
Morning, Maisie Mai.

701
01:11:12,573 --> 01:11:14,008
You just woke up?

702
01:11:14,041 --> 01:11:15,610
I thought you ran off.

703
01:11:15,643 --> 01:11:16,978
What?

704
01:11:17,011 --> 01:11:20,782
I just went into town,
paid off that tax bill.

705
01:11:20,815 --> 01:11:25,553
I stopped by the farm
store and got all this.

706
01:11:25,586 --> 01:11:27,088
What is it?

707
01:11:27,121 --> 01:11:33,194
New taps, Mason jars,
everything we need.

708
01:11:33,227 --> 01:11:36,097
I thought we might get
the sugarhouse going.

709
01:11:36,130 --> 01:11:38,065
Are you serious?

710
01:11:38,099 --> 01:11:39,734
I don't joke about syrup.

711
01:11:39,767 --> 01:11:43,070
[intriguing music]

712
01:12:02,123 --> 01:12:02,924
Come on.

713
01:12:31,252 --> 01:12:32,720
[tractor engine sputters]

714
01:12:32,753 --> 01:12:36,224
[cheers]

715
01:12:47,635 --> 01:12:51,138
[simmering]

716
01:13:22,502 --> 01:13:24,404
I've already put Cricket to bed.

717
01:13:24,437 --> 01:13:26,507
Yeah, she was worn out.

718
01:13:30,211 --> 01:13:32,346
So that's the last of it?

719
01:13:32,380 --> 01:13:34,615
Yeah, it's a
pretty good batch.

720
01:13:34,649 --> 01:13:38,753
We could sell a lot
come springtime.

721
01:13:38,786 --> 01:13:39,954
Are you gonna be around?

722
01:13:39,987 --> 01:13:42,890
[sighs]

723
01:13:44,825 --> 01:13:46,928
Being a brother
suits you, Finn.

724
01:13:52,033 --> 01:13:54,101
So why do you wanna leave?

725
01:13:54,135 --> 01:13:55,303
I just need to.

726
01:13:55,336 --> 01:13:56,837
That's not an answer.

727
01:14:00,141 --> 01:14:02,276
Look, I don't know
what to tell you.

728
01:14:02,310 --> 01:14:03,978
Tell me something.

729
01:14:07,348 --> 01:14:12,620
When I'm here,
I turn into him.

730
01:14:18,159 --> 01:14:19,060
Dad?

731
01:14:22,462 --> 01:14:23,531
What's wrong with that?

732
01:14:27,768 --> 01:14:30,104
Don't you see it?

733
01:14:30,137 --> 01:14:33,641
[grim music]

734
01:15:08,909 --> 01:15:12,380
[phone vibrating]

735
01:16:12,541 --> 01:16:13,908
Don't you want a drink?

736
01:16:19,747 --> 01:16:22,116
Look around, Darlene.

737
01:16:22,149 --> 01:16:26,187
[sighs] It's all
so pretty again.

738
01:16:26,220 --> 01:16:27,021
Don't you think?

739
01:16:36,130 --> 01:16:39,568
[ominous music]

740
01:16:57,084 --> 01:16:59,386
Cricket, you have
to take the pill.

741
01:16:59,420 --> 01:17:01,889
I don't want to.

742
01:17:01,922 --> 01:17:03,757
Please.

743
01:17:03,791 --> 01:17:04,658
No.

744
01:17:14,835 --> 01:17:17,771
[toilet flushes]

745
01:17:18,839 --> 01:17:22,710
Did you flush that
pill down the toilet?

746
01:17:22,743 --> 01:17:23,545
Sit down.

747
01:17:26,347 --> 01:17:27,882
These are expensive.

748
01:17:31,318 --> 01:17:32,086
[pills rattling]

749
01:17:32,119 --> 01:17:34,955
[door opens]

750
01:17:34,989 --> 01:17:35,923
[door closes]

751
01:17:35,956 --> 01:17:36,690
Open up.

752
01:17:36,724 --> 01:17:37,925
No.

753
01:17:37,958 --> 01:17:39,093
Please.

754
01:17:39,126 --> 01:17:40,027
No.

755
01:17:40,060 --> 01:17:41,061
MAISIE: You just have to--

756
01:17:41,095 --> 01:17:41,862
CRICKET: No.

757
01:17:41,896 --> 01:17:42,796
MAISIE: Yes, come on.

758
01:17:42,830 --> 01:17:43,598
CRICKET: No.

759
01:17:43,632 --> 01:17:44,899
Cricket, please.

760
01:17:44,932 --> 01:17:45,900
CRICKET: No.

761
01:17:48,603 --> 01:17:51,172
I cannot get her
to take this pill.

762
01:17:56,410 --> 01:17:57,612
Give me that.

763
01:18:02,116 --> 01:18:05,986
If you fetch my hat, I'll
throw this in the trash.

764
01:18:06,020 --> 01:18:07,656
Yes.

765
01:18:07,688 --> 01:18:09,990
Finn, that's her
seizure medicine.

766
01:18:26,407 --> 01:18:31,145
Now, finish that up.

767
01:18:31,178 --> 01:18:33,881
We've got a lot
of stuff to sell.

768
01:18:33,914 --> 01:18:37,351
[intriguing music]

769
01:18:52,601 --> 01:18:53,434
Hey, Cricket.

770
01:18:53,467 --> 01:18:54,235
How are you doing?

771
01:18:54,268 --> 01:18:55,035
Good.

772
01:18:55,069 --> 01:18:56,705
How's it going, Mr. Miller.

773
01:18:56,737 --> 01:18:57,639
Finn, how are you?

774
01:19:05,279 --> 01:19:07,047
Thank you, Mr. Miller.

775
01:19:11,385 --> 01:19:15,356
All right, thank you, kids.

776
01:19:15,389 --> 01:19:17,424
[chatter and laughter]

777
01:19:22,363 --> 01:19:25,065
[grim music]

778
01:19:36,944 --> 01:19:40,414
[knocking on door]

779
01:19:51,792 --> 01:19:53,628
Girl, you act like
you've seen a ghost.

780
01:19:53,662 --> 01:19:55,362
Dad.

781
01:19:55,396 --> 01:19:56,765
Maisie Mai.

782
01:19:56,797 --> 01:19:58,332
I knew you'd come back.

783
01:19:58,365 --> 01:19:59,900
How'd you know that?

784
01:19:59,933 --> 01:20:01,302
Because you're my dad.

785
01:20:01,335 --> 01:20:04,204
[chuckles]

786
01:20:05,072 --> 01:20:10,110
Finn, Cricket,
look who's here.

787
01:20:10,144 --> 01:20:12,681
Kept the place up.

788
01:20:12,714 --> 01:20:17,585
Cricket, you done all grown up.

789
01:20:17,619 --> 01:20:21,790
What are you, a
foot, 2 foot taller?

790
01:20:21,822 --> 01:20:24,659
[chuckles]

791
01:20:25,459 --> 01:20:26,260
Hm.

792
01:20:32,199 --> 01:20:33,500
Didn't expect to find you here.

793
01:20:33,535 --> 01:20:34,803
Why'd you leave?

794
01:20:37,572 --> 01:20:39,641
Always right to the business.

795
01:20:39,674 --> 01:20:41,776
We don't have
to talk about it.

796
01:20:41,810 --> 01:20:44,813
Dad's probably wanting
to get settled in.

797
01:20:44,845 --> 01:20:49,450
Nah, I don't
wanna bother y'all.

798
01:20:49,483 --> 01:20:52,319
I'll just sleep out in the shop.

799
01:20:52,353 --> 01:20:53,454
I need a few things.

800
01:21:01,796 --> 01:21:02,797
All grown up.

801
01:21:18,912 --> 01:21:21,014
Thought you were out west.

802
01:21:21,048 --> 01:21:23,083
Maisie needed me.

803
01:21:23,117 --> 01:21:24,753
Man of the house.

804
01:21:30,658 --> 01:21:31,458
Go ahead, boy.

805
01:21:36,430 --> 01:21:39,433
Ask what you wanna ask.

806
01:21:39,466 --> 01:21:42,403
You should tell all of us.

807
01:21:42,436 --> 01:21:47,842
You think they would
understand, like you and me?

808
01:21:53,715 --> 01:21:57,652
Look, I'm a no-good dog.

809
01:21:57,685 --> 01:22:01,488
Everybody knows that.

810
01:22:01,523 --> 01:22:04,759
[chuckles]

811
01:22:04,793 --> 01:22:07,629
Tell you what, boy, you
keep sleeping in my bed

812
01:22:07,662 --> 01:22:11,498
long enough, who knows
what you'll become?

813
01:22:17,337 --> 01:22:20,775
[water gushing]

814
01:22:30,618 --> 01:22:31,886
All right, let's pray.

815
01:22:36,791 --> 01:22:40,160
Lord, thank you for
this food and thank

816
01:22:40,194 --> 01:22:42,864
you for keeping cricket safe.

817
01:22:42,897 --> 01:22:46,033
I pray you can
always protect her.

818
01:22:46,066 --> 01:22:49,403
And Lord, please,
cheer up Darlene.

819
01:22:49,436 --> 01:22:52,941
She hasn't been doing
too well lately.

820
01:22:52,973 --> 01:22:55,844
And Lord, thank you
for bringing Dad home

821
01:22:55,877 --> 01:22:58,212
so we can all be a family again.

822
01:22:58,245 --> 01:22:59,046
Amen.

823
01:23:08,288 --> 01:23:10,057
Are you sure you don't
need another blanket?

824
01:23:10,090 --> 01:23:13,026
Uh, no, hon, I sleep hot.

825
01:23:13,060 --> 01:23:14,762
Good night, Dad.

826
01:23:14,796 --> 01:23:15,730
Yep.

827
01:23:18,666 --> 01:23:22,169
[pensive music]

828
01:23:27,140 --> 01:23:30,612
[insects chirping]

829
01:23:55,469 --> 01:23:58,907
[laughter]

830
01:23:58,940 --> 01:24:01,910
[chatter]

831
01:24:05,212 --> 01:24:07,715
Did you get your
snapshot taken?

832
01:24:07,749 --> 01:24:10,150
I couldn't get
Cricket to hold still.

833
01:24:10,183 --> 01:24:13,688
Hey, boy, think you could,
uh, give me a ride into town?

834
01:24:13,721 --> 01:24:14,522
No trucks.

835
01:24:14,556 --> 01:24:15,890
You just ain't gonna make it.

836
01:24:15,924 --> 01:24:17,692
Can I get out of this shirt?

837
01:24:17,725 --> 01:24:20,227
A little dirt
never hurt nothing.

838
01:24:20,260 --> 01:24:22,296
Come on.

839
01:24:22,329 --> 01:24:23,130
Let's go.

840
01:24:27,434 --> 01:24:30,572
See you for dinner.

841
01:24:30,605 --> 01:24:33,307
[chatter]

842
01:24:35,309 --> 01:24:38,780
[dogs barking]

843
01:24:42,249 --> 01:24:44,786
GAGE: Hey, boy.

844
01:24:44,819 --> 01:24:47,387
What is this?

845
01:24:47,421 --> 01:24:48,723
Baby Jesus.

846
01:24:52,326 --> 01:24:55,663
[bottles clinking]

847
01:25:12,179 --> 01:25:15,683
You remember this, don't you?

848
01:25:15,717 --> 01:25:18,753
Yeah.

849
01:25:18,786 --> 01:25:22,890
I reckon the whole town does.

850
01:25:22,924 --> 01:25:24,257
What was it?

851
01:25:24,291 --> 01:25:26,259
Little league championship game?

852
01:25:26,293 --> 01:25:30,898
And you released how many
mice onto this field?

853
01:25:30,932 --> 01:25:31,733
80.

854
01:25:31,766 --> 01:25:34,368
80.

855
01:25:34,401 --> 01:25:35,335
80 mice.

856
01:25:39,040 --> 01:25:41,109
Didn't some of them jump
over into that concession

857
01:25:41,141 --> 01:25:41,909
stand over there?

858
01:25:41,943 --> 01:25:45,245
[laughs] Yeah.

859
01:25:45,278 --> 01:25:47,715
They had to rescue one of
them from the cheese sauce.

860
01:25:47,749 --> 01:25:50,685
[both laughing]

861
01:25:55,089 --> 01:25:58,358
You, uh-- you
remember the first

862
01:25:58,392 --> 01:26:02,764
thing that I said to the cops
when I come and pick you up?

863
01:26:02,797 --> 01:26:06,233
[pensive music]

864
01:26:07,702 --> 01:26:09,570
"Where's my little outlaw?"

865
01:26:12,106 --> 01:26:13,141
Little outlaw.

866
01:26:25,153 --> 01:26:26,954
I'll take one of those.

867
01:26:40,333 --> 01:26:43,771
[car engine humming]

868
01:26:43,805 --> 01:26:45,272
[car door opens]

869
01:26:45,305 --> 01:26:48,776
[laughter and chatter]

870
01:26:50,778 --> 01:26:53,748
[knocking]

871
01:26:56,718 --> 01:26:59,319
GAGE: What was that?

872
01:26:59,352 --> 01:27:02,790
[laughter]

873
01:27:26,581 --> 01:27:30,250
I wonder if you have colic.

874
01:27:30,283 --> 01:27:31,719
Has your stomach
been hurting you?

875
01:27:48,136 --> 01:27:49,837
I feel like everything's
coming undone.

876
01:27:53,373 --> 01:27:56,811
Dad and Finn, all they wanna do
is hang out in the sugarhouse.

877
01:27:59,379 --> 01:28:01,082
They hardly have time for us.

878
01:28:01,115 --> 01:28:03,918
[donkey snorts]

879
01:28:05,653 --> 01:28:06,453
But I have you.

880
01:28:09,524 --> 01:28:10,625
I'll always have you.

881
01:28:15,963 --> 01:28:18,866
[footsteps]

882
01:28:20,333 --> 01:28:21,301
GAGE: Girls.

883
01:28:41,488 --> 01:28:43,724
Dad.

884
01:28:43,758 --> 01:28:45,626
Yep.

885
01:28:45,660 --> 01:28:48,863
Darlene hasn't been
feeling too well lately.

886
01:28:48,896 --> 01:28:51,866
She barely drinks her water,
and all she wants to do

887
01:28:51,899 --> 01:28:54,135
is lay in the grass.

888
01:28:54,168 --> 01:28:57,772
Well, she is old.

889
01:28:57,805 --> 01:29:00,407
But I'm worried about her.

890
01:29:00,440 --> 01:29:03,845
Do you think we could have a vet
come out and take a look at her?

891
01:29:03,878 --> 01:29:05,345
There was that one
friend of Mom's.

892
01:29:09,517 --> 01:29:14,655
No, I don't want her out here.

893
01:29:22,163 --> 01:29:27,168
It's hard, isn't it,
watching her suffer?

894
01:29:32,340 --> 01:29:33,140
Tell you what.

895
01:29:36,310 --> 01:29:38,512
I'll have somebody come out.

896
01:29:38,546 --> 01:29:39,780
Thanks, Dad.

897
01:29:39,814 --> 01:29:40,681
You bet.

898
01:29:45,019 --> 01:29:48,388
Did you take
your pill, Cricket?

899
01:29:48,421 --> 01:29:49,422
Mm-hmm.

900
01:30:35,836 --> 01:30:39,307
[door hinges creaking]

901
01:30:39,340 --> 01:30:42,810
[somber music]

902
01:31:13,274 --> 01:31:14,275
Maisie.

903
01:31:14,308 --> 01:31:17,244
[gasps]

904
01:31:19,213 --> 01:31:22,917
You need to get out of
here before Dad comes back.

905
01:31:22,950 --> 01:31:24,652
Do you know about this?

906
01:31:24,685 --> 01:31:27,688
You just need to
leave it lie, OK?

907
01:31:27,722 --> 01:31:32,126
What would happen if
Cricket came in here?

908
01:31:32,159 --> 01:31:33,561
We have to call the police.

909
01:31:33,594 --> 01:31:34,962
You can't call the police.

910
01:31:34,996 --> 01:31:35,763
Why not?

911
01:31:35,796 --> 01:31:37,398
Because he's my dad.

912
01:31:37,431 --> 01:31:40,968
He's my dad too.

913
01:31:41,002 --> 01:31:42,470
What?

914
01:31:42,502 --> 01:31:46,007
[sighs]

915
01:31:47,274 --> 01:31:48,142
What?

916
01:32:05,726 --> 01:32:07,194
He's not your dad.

917
01:32:10,498 --> 01:32:14,769
After me, Mom couldn't
have kids anymore.

918
01:32:18,039 --> 01:32:19,473
She always wanted a girl.

919
01:32:22,810 --> 01:32:24,245
You're not my brother?

920
01:32:27,982 --> 01:32:29,183
I'm sorry.

921
01:32:48,335 --> 01:32:49,303
[insects chirping]

922
01:32:49,336 --> 01:32:52,773
[dog barking]

923
01:33:05,119 --> 01:33:07,755
So where are the girls?

924
01:33:15,729 --> 01:33:16,531
What?

925
01:33:19,433 --> 01:33:20,634
I'll look upstairs.

926
01:33:40,754 --> 01:33:41,755
Are you coming to dinner?

927
01:33:41,789 --> 01:33:42,656
No.

928
01:33:47,862 --> 01:33:49,230
What if I make pie?

929
01:33:49,263 --> 01:33:51,031
Will you please shut the door?

930
01:33:57,505 --> 01:34:00,407
(WHISPERING)
Always take the pie.

931
01:34:00,441 --> 01:34:03,844
[pensive music]

932
01:34:05,312 --> 01:34:08,716
[insects chirping]

933
01:34:14,589 --> 01:34:17,391
Did Mom ever talk
to you about me?

934
01:34:23,364 --> 01:34:28,702
Do you think she loved me
the same way I love you?

935
01:34:39,380 --> 01:34:42,850
[tools clinking]

936
01:34:58,199 --> 01:35:01,636
[chatter]

937
01:35:13,047 --> 01:35:13,981
[car engine starts]

938
01:35:14,014 --> 01:35:15,950
I gotta check on something.

939
01:35:39,106 --> 01:35:42,042
[cricket crying]

940
01:35:42,076 --> 01:35:43,877
Cricket, don't look at it.

941
01:35:48,749 --> 01:35:49,584
Don't look at it.

942
01:35:52,219 --> 01:35:53,020
Don't look at it.

943
01:35:56,490 --> 01:35:59,960
[car engine humming]

944
01:36:06,900 --> 01:36:07,768
Let's go see Darlene.

945
01:36:16,611 --> 01:36:18,647
Let's get this
thing out of here.

946
01:36:18,680 --> 01:36:22,182
[gentle music]

947
01:36:33,695 --> 01:36:34,596
Darlene.

948
01:36:40,301 --> 01:36:41,935
Darlene!

949
01:36:41,969 --> 01:36:43,871
No, no, no, no, no.

950
01:36:43,904 --> 01:36:44,838
Maisie.

951
01:36:44,872 --> 01:36:46,574
Hold on, Maisie.

952
01:36:46,608 --> 01:36:47,642
Hold on, Maisie.

953
01:36:47,676 --> 01:36:49,877
Hold on!

954
01:36:49,910 --> 01:36:50,844
No!

955
01:36:55,416 --> 01:36:56,183
[door slams]

956
01:36:56,216 --> 01:36:58,018
Get that out of here!

957
01:37:06,327 --> 01:37:09,798
[car engine starts]

958
01:37:11,298 --> 01:37:12,933
Maisie.

959
01:37:12,966 --> 01:37:14,034
Maisie, It's OK.
Maisie--

960
01:37:14,068 --> 01:37:14,935
Get off of me.

961
01:37:19,406 --> 01:37:20,207
No!

962
01:37:23,712 --> 01:37:27,214
[sobbing]

963
01:37:37,224 --> 01:37:38,225
Maisie.

964
01:37:43,030 --> 01:37:45,165
You killed her!

965
01:37:45,199 --> 01:37:47,034
She-- she was sick.

966
01:37:47,067 --> 01:37:47,868
Ask the vet.

967
01:37:47,901 --> 01:37:49,002
No, you're lying!

968
01:37:49,036 --> 01:37:51,939
She just needed help,
but you killed her!

969
01:37:51,972 --> 01:37:53,842
It was the right thing to do.

970
01:37:53,874 --> 01:37:55,976
Nothing you do is right!

971
01:38:00,582 --> 01:38:02,784
I know what you're
doing in the sugarhouse.

972
01:38:08,021 --> 01:38:11,392
You think you know something.

973
01:38:11,425 --> 01:38:13,862
I think the police
might wanna know.

974
01:38:16,397 --> 01:38:19,567
My own daughter
couldn't turn me in.

975
01:38:19,601 --> 01:38:20,467
What'd you call me?

976
01:38:25,372 --> 01:38:27,474
I see who you are now.

977
01:38:53,100 --> 01:38:56,538
[car engine sputters]

978
01:38:59,006 --> 01:39:00,374
Come on!

979
01:39:12,219 --> 01:39:13,153
Maisie!

980
01:39:28,837 --> 01:39:31,438
I need you to bury
this down at the gully!

981
01:39:31,472 --> 01:39:33,340
What's going on?

982
01:39:33,373 --> 01:39:34,943
Fetch a shovel!

983
01:39:34,975 --> 01:39:35,777
Go!

984
01:39:40,214 --> 01:39:42,817
[water flowing]

985
01:39:47,154 --> 01:39:48,155
That's everything.

986
01:39:48,188 --> 01:39:50,157
Bury it and meet
me at the truck.

987
01:39:50,190 --> 01:39:51,693
Is it the cops?

988
01:39:51,726 --> 01:39:52,961
Just get it done!

989
01:39:58,365 --> 01:40:01,870
[car engine humming]

990
01:40:13,615 --> 01:40:14,414
Let's go.

991
01:40:22,757 --> 01:40:26,226
[solemn music]

992
01:40:29,731 --> 01:40:33,233
[door opens]

993
01:41:28,623 --> 01:41:32,092
[tires screech]

994
01:42:05,526 --> 01:42:09,030
[car engine humming]

995
01:42:26,014 --> 01:42:28,315
You good?

996
01:42:28,348 --> 01:42:29,617
I'm good.

997
01:42:29,651 --> 01:42:34,022
No, are you good?

998
01:42:34,055 --> 01:42:35,222
I'm good.

999
01:42:41,161 --> 01:42:42,295
You never know.

1000
01:42:57,444 --> 01:43:00,915
[door hinges squeal]

1001
01:43:02,416 --> 01:43:08,856
[cackles] Look at you
back from the grave.

1002
01:43:08,890 --> 01:43:10,792
GAGE: I have a lap
or two left in me.

1003
01:43:10,825 --> 01:43:14,428
Bet you're gonna need
him in this racket.

1004
01:43:14,461 --> 01:43:16,164
Talk to Mosley?

1005
01:43:16,229 --> 01:43:17,532
What do you want me to say?

1006
01:43:17,565 --> 01:43:19,167
How about don't shoot me?

1007
01:43:19,199 --> 01:43:22,570
[cackling]

1008
01:43:28,810 --> 01:43:29,977
Schmitz?

1009
01:43:30,011 --> 01:43:31,145
Who's Schmitz?

1010
01:43:33,715 --> 01:43:35,315
Ah, you're kidding me, right?

1011
01:43:35,348 --> 01:43:37,350
GAGE: That's my boy, Finn.

1012
01:43:37,384 --> 01:43:39,954
I told you about him.

1013
01:43:39,987 --> 01:43:42,422
Well, Schmitz here tried
to pull a gun on me once.

1014
01:43:42,456 --> 01:43:43,891
Well, you probably
had it coming.

1015
01:43:43,925 --> 01:43:45,860
Maybe I did.

1016
01:43:45,893 --> 01:43:47,895
Come on, Quaid.

1017
01:43:47,929 --> 01:43:49,030
He's one of us.

1018
01:43:55,268 --> 01:43:57,437
You look like a Schmitz to me.

1019
01:44:05,913 --> 01:44:08,583
[clacking]

1020
01:44:09,382 --> 01:44:10,250
Cricket.

1021
01:44:30,171 --> 01:44:30,972
Cricket.

1022
01:44:31,005 --> 01:44:31,806
Finn!

1023
01:44:39,180 --> 01:44:39,947
It's OK.

1024
01:44:39,981 --> 01:44:42,183
It's OK.

1025
01:44:42,216 --> 01:44:44,752
Will you just
please make it stop.

1026
01:44:44,786 --> 01:44:46,254
Please, make it stop.

1027
01:44:46,286 --> 01:44:49,757
[somber music]

1028
01:45:05,239 --> 01:45:08,743
[footsteps]

1029
01:45:29,197 --> 01:45:32,834
You know who you're
asking about that?

1030
01:45:32,867 --> 01:45:34,569
Hey, boy.

1031
01:45:34,602 --> 01:45:37,370
Floyd and I are gonna go
take care of something.

1032
01:45:37,404 --> 01:45:40,440
Why don't you hang out here
with old Quaid for a bit?

1033
01:45:40,473 --> 01:45:41,341
Dad.

1034
01:45:52,485 --> 01:45:55,056
I don't trust this guy.

1035
01:45:55,089 --> 01:45:56,657
You shouldn't.

1036
01:46:11,606 --> 01:46:14,141
I'm gonna sit over there, and
I'm gonna stare at that wall

1037
01:46:14,175 --> 01:46:15,475
until my dad comes back.

1038
01:46:33,895 --> 01:46:37,365
[solemn music]

1039
01:46:40,868 --> 01:46:44,872
I love you to the moon.

1040
01:46:44,906 --> 01:46:46,641
I love you to the stars.

1041
01:46:58,886 --> 01:47:01,122
Hey, Schmitz.

1042
01:47:01,155 --> 01:47:03,958
Want to kill some time?

1043
01:47:03,991 --> 01:47:06,093
I want none of that.

1044
01:47:06,127 --> 01:47:08,863
I hear different.

1045
01:47:08,896 --> 01:47:11,799
In fact, I hear you
may be slinging.

1046
01:47:11,832 --> 01:47:12,667
See?

1047
01:47:12,700 --> 01:47:14,235
Yeah, you better
be careful, son.

1048
01:47:14,268 --> 01:47:15,369
That's the old man's work.

1049
01:47:18,172 --> 01:47:19,439
Unless you want a piece of it.

1050
01:47:23,611 --> 01:47:26,614
Come on, boy, look here.

1051
01:47:26,647 --> 01:47:30,051
[sniffs] So much more
where that came from.

1052
01:47:30,084 --> 01:47:32,386
We're gonna ring that
bell, you and me.

1053
01:47:32,420 --> 01:47:34,055
What are you talking about?

1054
01:47:34,088 --> 01:47:37,091
You don't know?

1055
01:47:37,124 --> 01:47:39,160
Daddy's done, son.

1056
01:47:39,193 --> 01:47:40,661
This is our time.

1057
01:47:40,695 --> 01:47:42,596
I don't want nothing
to do with you.

1058
01:47:50,871 --> 01:47:52,606
You just might.

1059
01:47:57,445 --> 01:47:59,880
Pretty little girls back home--

1060
01:47:59,914 --> 01:48:01,048
I'm just saying.

1061
01:48:01,082 --> 01:48:02,616
Gonna need taken care of.

1062
01:48:02,650 --> 01:48:04,484
How about I take care of you?

1063
01:48:04,518 --> 01:48:08,723
[cackles] Whoa, boy,
you got some hate on you.

1064
01:48:15,363 --> 01:48:17,698
All right, I'll run with it.

1065
01:48:22,436 --> 01:48:24,605
Hey, what do you
mean, my dad's done?

1066
01:48:27,675 --> 01:48:30,678
Figure it out, kid.

1067
01:48:30,711 --> 01:48:33,581
I know how to get to him.

1068
01:48:33,614 --> 01:48:34,849
I know how to get to all y'all.

1069
01:48:34,882 --> 01:48:38,185
[grim music]

1070
01:48:52,900 --> 01:48:53,834
Hey!

1071
01:49:03,677 --> 01:49:07,114
[gun cocks]

1072
01:49:11,552 --> 01:49:15,489
[scoffs]

1073
01:49:18,426 --> 01:49:22,396
Gage, in here.

1074
01:49:22,430 --> 01:49:24,465
What'd I tell you?

1075
01:49:24,498 --> 01:49:26,434
Took him long enough.

1076
01:49:26,467 --> 01:49:27,201
You got it.

1077
01:49:27,234 --> 01:49:28,502
My boy got it.

1078
01:49:28,537 --> 01:49:30,638
QUAID: The little girl back
home is more intimidating.

1079
01:49:30,671 --> 01:49:33,107
But you were happy
about tonight, didn't you?

1080
01:49:33,140 --> 01:49:35,676
Put that thing down, man.

1081
01:49:35,709 --> 01:49:39,080
It's not even loaded.

1082
01:49:39,113 --> 01:49:41,715
[chatter]

1083
01:49:41,749 --> 01:49:44,785
What is this?

1084
01:49:44,819 --> 01:49:47,154
It's your baptism.

1085
01:49:47,188 --> 01:49:49,023
Into what?

1086
01:49:49,056 --> 01:49:52,660
Into all this.

1087
01:49:52,693 --> 01:49:53,761
Don't you see it?

1088
01:49:59,500 --> 01:50:04,071
Come down to it, you would
have stopped him, wouldn't you?

1089
01:50:04,105 --> 01:50:05,473
I wasn't thinking.

1090
01:50:05,506 --> 01:50:06,541
That's right.

1091
01:50:09,410 --> 01:50:12,547
Knew what your blood told you.

1092
01:50:12,581 --> 01:50:17,885
There was a time when you
belonged to your mother,

1093
01:50:17,918 --> 01:50:20,387
same as me.

1094
01:50:20,421 --> 01:50:27,194
And when she died, you and
I, we took on this new blood.

1095
01:50:27,228 --> 01:50:29,697
Don't you understand?

1096
01:50:29,730 --> 01:50:34,502
This here, this is what we are.

1097
01:50:39,541 --> 01:50:41,675
You are my son.

1098
01:50:54,455 --> 01:50:55,656
[bottles clink]

1099
01:50:55,689 --> 01:50:58,225
[chatter]

1100
01:51:21,949 --> 01:51:25,419
[insects chirping]

1101
01:51:41,769 --> 01:51:45,139
Dear God, thank
you for this food.

1102
01:51:45,172 --> 01:51:48,510
I pray Darlene gets lots
to eat in heaven right now.

1103
01:51:48,543 --> 01:51:51,178
And God, thank
you for my sister,

1104
01:51:51,212 --> 01:51:55,049
Maisie, who loves me and
takes good care of me.

1105
01:51:55,082 --> 01:51:58,620
And God, please bring Finn home.

1106
01:51:58,653 --> 01:51:59,453
Amen.

1107
01:52:13,300 --> 01:52:16,770
[door hinges squeal]

1108
01:52:30,719 --> 01:52:33,187
[solemn music]

1109
01:52:33,220 --> 01:52:37,191
[sighs]

1110
01:53:49,897 --> 01:53:53,400
[sighs]

1111
01:55:21,523 --> 01:55:25,025
[sighs]

1112
01:55:30,497 --> 01:55:32,232
What did I do wrong?

1113
01:55:51,018 --> 01:55:52,352
Why can't I hear you?

1114
01:56:02,129 --> 01:56:05,633
[birdsong]

1115
01:56:41,970 --> 01:56:45,472
[phone vibrating, ringing]

1116
01:56:47,474 --> 01:56:48,442
Finn.

1117
01:56:48,475 --> 01:56:51,078
Maisie, the birds--

1118
01:56:51,111 --> 01:56:52,446
they're everywhere.

1119
01:56:52,479 --> 01:56:55,048
I hear them too.

1120
01:56:55,082 --> 01:56:55,984
Where are you?

1121
01:56:56,016 --> 01:56:57,986
The masonry.

1122
01:56:58,018 --> 01:56:59,787
I can't get away.

1123
01:56:59,821 --> 01:57:01,321
MAISIE (ON PHONE): I'll come.

1124
01:57:01,355 --> 01:57:03,892
Maisie, don't come.

1125
01:57:03,925 --> 01:57:05,627
It's not safe.

1126
01:57:05,660 --> 01:57:09,597
Finn, I'm bringing you home.

1127
01:57:09,631 --> 01:57:13,100
[birdsong]

1128
01:57:57,645 --> 01:58:04,217
Finn, why'd you run off?

1129
01:58:07,855 --> 01:58:11,124
I just had to get out.

1130
01:58:11,158 --> 01:58:13,260
Well, we have to go.

1131
01:58:28,910 --> 01:58:30,344
Son.

1132
01:58:30,377 --> 01:58:31,211
Finn.

1133
01:58:34,214 --> 01:58:36,316
Finn, come with me now.

1134
01:58:36,350 --> 01:58:38,151
It's no place for you, Maisie.

1135
01:58:38,185 --> 01:58:39,587
It's no place for him either.

1136
01:58:42,757 --> 01:58:44,324
Hold on.

1137
01:58:44,358 --> 01:58:47,260
Stop!

1138
01:58:47,294 --> 01:58:50,230
Please!

1139
01:58:50,263 --> 01:58:53,735
[pensive music]

1140
01:59:00,775 --> 01:59:01,576
Sorry.

1141
01:59:04,579 --> 01:59:05,513
Could have done better.

1142
01:59:05,546 --> 01:59:08,983
[distant siren wails]

1143
01:59:17,892 --> 01:59:26,333
Dad, I left the drugs in
the truck and the gun too.

1144
01:59:26,366 --> 01:59:30,303
Well, good thinking.

1145
01:59:32,940 --> 01:59:37,244
You were just doing what
your blood told you.

1146
01:59:44,418 --> 01:59:45,285
I'll take care of it.

1147
01:59:48,856 --> 01:59:49,757
Dad!

1148
02:00:06,373 --> 02:00:07,274
Yep.

1149
02:00:20,353 --> 02:00:21,689
Let's get you home.

1150
02:00:28,996 --> 02:00:32,466
[intriguing music]

1151
02:00:58,760 --> 02:01:02,230
[insects chirping]

1152
02:01:05,733 --> 02:01:09,203
[laughter and chatter]

1153
02:01:32,425 --> 02:01:34,294
Hey, look what I got.

1154
02:02:18,405 --> 02:02:21,776
[theme music]